  "production_date": "probably 1460s",
  "brief_description": "The Virgin, holding the infant Christ, sits on a marble throne with a luxurious material stretched across it -- a cloth of honour, something often hung behind medieval royalty. Two saints stand with them, and particular symbols tell us who they are. On the Virgin's right is Saint Peter, a key peeping out from beneath his green robe. On the Virgin's left is Saint Paul, the sword by which he was martyred leaning against the arch behind him. He holds out a delicate flower to Christ: a carnation. Carnations or pinks were known in northern Europe as 'nail flowers' because their scent resembles cloves, which are shaped like the nails driven into Christ's body when he was crucified. So this little flower may allude to the Passion (Christ's torture and crucifixion).",
